course: books
lesson: 93
title: Just So Stories, The Cat that Walked by Himself, part 1: the First Friend and the First Servant
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Long ago all the tame animals were wild: Dog, Horse, Cow, Sheep and Pig walked in the Wet Wild Woods, and the wildest of all was the Cat, who walked by himself, and all places were alike to him. The Man was wild too, until the Woman made a home: a dry Cave, clean sand on the floor, a fire at the back and a horse-skin curtain over the door.
explain: After a fine supper the Woman looks at a mutton blade-bone and makes the First Singing Magic. The wild animals see the fire and wonder. Wild Dog smells roast mutton and goes to look; the Cat says «Nenni!», he will not come, but he follows secretly and hides where he can hear everything.
explain: The Woman throws Wild Dog a roast bone, and he wants more. She offers him as many bones as he needs if he helps the Man hunt by day and guards the Cave at night. He agrees and lays his head in her lap. He is no longer Wild Dog but the First Friend.
explain: Next night the Woman dries sweet grass, plaits a halter and makes the Second Singing Magic. Wild Horse comes for the grass, bends his head for the halter and becomes the First Servant, to carry them from place to place. Each time the Cat listens, says the animal is foolish, walks home by his wild lone, and tells nobody.
fact: «He walked by himself, and all places were alike to him» is said of the Cat again and again, like a chorus.
fact: «By their wild lones» is Kipling's playful way of saying all alone; «befell and behappened» means it happened.
fact: The Woman «picked out a nice dry Cave, instead of a heap of wet leaves», strewed clean sand, lit a fire and hung «a dried wild-horse skin, tail-down», saying «Wipe you feet, dear, when you come in, and now we'll keep house.»
fact: Supper: wild sheep roasted on hot stones, wild duck stuffed with wild rice, marrow-bones of wild oxen, wild cherries and grenadillas.
fact: The Woman looks at «the big fat blade-bone» of a shoulder of mutton and makes «the First Singing Magic in the world».
fact: «Nenni!» means «No!» (an old French-sounding word); the Cat says «I am the Cat who walks by himself, and all places are alike to me. I will not come.»
fact: Wild Dog calls her «O my Enemy and Wife of my Enemy»; after the bone he says «O my Friend and Wife of my Friend».
fact: The bargain: «help my Man to hunt through the day and guard this Cave at night, and I will give you as many roast bones as you need.» The Woman names him «the First Friend».
fact: Wild Horse comes «tripping and stumbling on his long mane» and asks «where is Wild Dog?»; the Woman laughs and says he came not for Wild Dog but «for the sake of this good grass». He may eat the wonderful grass three times a day, and he becomes «the First Servant», to «carry us from place to place».
fact: The Cat each time: «This is a very wise Woman, but she is not so wise as I am» and «That is a very foolish Dog» (or Horse). «But he never told anybody.»
fact: Real life: dogs were the first animals people tamed, from wolves, at least 15,000 years ago, while people were still hunters, long before farming.
fact: Real life: horses were tamed much later, about 5,500 years ago on the grasslands of Central Asia, after cows, sheep, goats and pigs, which were tamed about 10,000 years ago; Kipling's order, Dog then Horse then Cow, is not the real one.
fact: Real life: reading marks on shoulder blades to tell the future was a real old custom in many lands; in ancient China such «oracle bones» carry some of the oldest Chinese writing. The magic itself did not work.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gyatt» = a word kids shout when surprised, without much meaning; «bummer» (older US) = what a shame; «tara-a-bit» (Black Country, England) = see you later; «alright my lover» (West Country) = hello, friend.
exercise: Which animal is «the wildest of all the wild animals»? | The Cat | The wild Dog | The Horse | The Pig, rooting about in the Wet Wild Woods
exercise: What does the Cat always say about places? | All places are alike to him | He likes the Cave best of all because it is warm and dry inside | He hates the woods | He wants a house
exercise: Who stops the Man living in his wild ways? | The Woman | The Dog | The Eldest Magician, who taught the animals their plays | The Cat
exercise: What does the Woman pick instead of a heap of wet leaves? | A nice dry Cave | A tree-house high in the Wet Wild Woods, away from the wolves | A tent | A canoe
exercise: What does she hang across the Cave's opening? | A dried wild-horse skin, tail-down | A curtain woven from wild grass and wild rice | A wooden door | Nothing at all
exercise: What does she tell the Man when he comes in? | «Wipe you feet, dear» | «Bring more wood for the fire, it is getting cold» | «Go back out» | «Feed the Dog»
exercise: What does the Woman look at to make her Magic? | A mutton blade-bone | A round stone from the river, with a hole worn right through it | The Moon | The fire only
exercise: What is the first Magic called? | The First Singing Magic in the world | The Still Magic | The Great Magic of the Djinn | The Little Magic
exercise: What does Wild Dog smell? | Roast mutton | Wild garlic and wild pepper on the hot stones of the fire | Milk | Rain
exercise: What does the Cat say when Dog asks him to come? | «Nenni!» | «Go away, Dog!» | «Yes, I will come!» | «Wait for me, I am coming too»
exercise: What does the Cat do after refusing? | Follows softly and hides to listen | Goes off to the far side of the Wet Wild Woods and sleeps all night in a hollow tree | Tells the Horse | Runs into the Cave first
exercise: What does the Woman first give Wild Dog? | A roasted mutton-bone | A bowl of warm white milk with honey in it | Some grass | A halter
exercise: What must the Dog do for as many bones as he needs? | Help hunt by day and guard the Cave at night | Carry the Woman and the Man from place to place on his back | Give milk | Catch mice
exercise: How does Wild Dog's greeting change after the bone? | From «my Enemy» to «my Friend» | From «my Friend» to «my Enemy», because he wants more bones and she says no | It doesn't change | He stops talking
exercise: What does the Woman name him? | The First Friend | The First Servant | The Giver of Good Food | The Cat's best Friend
exercise: What does the Woman make for the Horse on the second night? | Dried grass and a plaited horse-hide halter | A saddle and a bit, like the Horse in the Camel story | A bone | A bed
exercise: How does Wild Horse come up to the Cave? | Tripping and stumbling on his long mane | Galloping proudly with his wild head held high | Swimming | Hopping
exercise: What does the Woman say Horse really came for? | The good grass | To find Wild Dog, his old friend from the Wet Wild Woods | To see the fire | To eat bones
exercise: How often may Horse eat the wonderful grass? | Three times a day | Just once every week | At any time he likes | Only on a Sunday morning
exercise: What is Horse's new name? | The First Servant | The First Friend of all | The Carrier of Good Things | The Wild Thing of the Woods
exercise: What does the Cat say about the Dog and the Horse after their bargains? | That each is very foolish | That each is very wise and he will do the same tomorrow | Nothing | That they are his friends
exercise: Whom does the Cat tell about what he heard? | Nobody | All the wild animals | The Bat | The Woman
exercise: Looking back: in the Camel story, which three animals worked for the Man? | The Horse, the Dog and the Ox | The Cat, the Cow and the Sheep | The Horse, the Camel and the Djinn | The Dog, the Pig and the Ox
test: Freya: «the Cat went to the Cave with the Dog and they both made a deal, gyatt.» (gyatt = something kids shout) Put it right. | The Cat refused, followed secretly and made no deal | Right, they both asked for bones | The Cat went first | The Dog stayed home
test: Why does Wild Dog become the «First Friend»? | He agrees to help hunt and guard, for roast bones | The Woman catches him in a net and keeps him | He is the Cat's friend | He brings milk
test: The Cat says the Woman is wise «but she is not so wise as I am». What does this show about him? | He is proud and thinks he is cleverest | He is shy | He wants to be her servant | He is frightened of her
test: What would happen if the Cat had gone in with the Dog? | Perhaps he'd have got a bargain too | Nothing different at all | The Woman would have chased the Dog out | The Horse would have stayed wild
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Cat secretly listens to both bargains | The Cat tells the other wild animals all about it | The Horse is the First Friend | The Man makes the Magic
test: Jack from Somerset: «alright my lover, horses were tamed first, before dogs.» (alright my lover = hello, friend) Put it right. | Dogs came first, at least 15,000 years ago; horses much later | Right, horses came first | Both on the same day | Cats came first
test: Real life: where did dogs come from? | They were tamed from wolves | They were tamed from foxes, which is why foxes and dogs look alike | From cats | They were always tame
test: Real life: about when were horses tamed? | About 5,500 years ago | A million years ago, when people first walked the Earth | Last century | Before dogs
test: Real life: Kipling has Dog, then Horse, then Cow. What is closer to the true order? | Dog first; cows, sheep and pigs; horses much later | Horse, Dog, Cow | Cow, Horse, Dog | Cat, Dog, Horse
test: Kayla: «bummer, the blade-bone magic's real, my uncle reads bones.» (bummer = what a shame) What is true? | Bone-reading was a real old custom, but it does not tell the future | It works every time | It is how dogs were tamed | Only cats can do it
test: Looking back: which earlier story also had a Man who left an idle animal alone and made others work «double-time»? | How the Camel Got His Hump | The Beginning of the Armadillos | The Elephant's Child | The Crab that Played with the Sea
test: Looking back: in the Crab story, which animal slipped away to play alone, like the Cat here? | Pau Amma the Crab | All-the-Elephant-there-was | The Rat of the Moon | All-the-Cow-there-was
test: Looking back: who in the Taffy stories lived in a Cave and kept house too? | Teshumai Tewindrow, Taffy's Mummy | Mother Jaguar | Balkis | The Stranger-man
test: Looking back: what did Taffy draw at the Cave mouth in her picture-letter? | Her Mummy with her hands up | A Cat | A horse-skin curtain | A fire
test: Ted from Dudley: «tara-a-bit, I'm off to read how the Cat got his name First Friend.» (tara-a-bit = see you later) Put it right. | The Dog is the First Friend; the Cat stays the one who walks by himself | Right, the Cat is the First Friend | The Cat is the First Servant | The Horse is the First Friend
test: ★★★ Careful: on the second night, why does the Woman laugh at Wild Horse's question «where is Wild Dog?» | She knows he really came for the grass, not for the Dog | Wild Dog is hiding behind her | She thinks horses are funny | She doesn't know
test: ★★★ Counting: in this part, how many Singing Magics has the Woman made, and how many animals have made bargains? | Two Magics, two bargains | One Magic, two bargains | Two Magics, three bargains | Three Magics, three bargains
test: ★★★ «O my Enemy and Wife of my Enemy», says Wild Dog first. Whom does he mean by «my Enemy»? | The Man | The hidden Cat | The Woman's father | The fire
exam: Identify the condition imposed by the Woman upon Wild Dog in exchange for roast bones. | That he assist the Man in hunting by day and guard the Cave by night | That he give milk thrice daily | That he carry the household from place to place | That he catch mice
exam: Ascertain the designation conferred upon Wild Horse following his acceptance of the halter. | The First Servant | The First Friend of Man | The Giver of Good Food | The Cat that Walks by Himself
exam: It is given that the Cat followed each animal to the Cave. Select the response most consistent with his subsequent conduct. | He returned alone to the Woods and informed nobody | He reported everything to the Bat | He entered the Cave forthwith | He warned Wild Horse
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the domestication of the dog in the real world? | It was tamed from the wolf, at least fifteen thousand years ago | It was tamed from the fox in the last century | It was the final animal to be tamed | It was never tamed
